text:
kevin_support: Heads-up — customer on prod reported a sudden billing spike and duplicate downstream order creations. They trace it to our Node TS SDK retries when requests hit a proxy timeout. Any known undici/fetch AbortController interaction?

maria_sre: Can you paste the SDK version + minimal repro? Also what Node version and undici version if pinned?

kevin_support: SDK @ 1.11.0, Node 18.15, undici 5.15 (pulled by SDK). Repro attached from customer (sanitized):
```ts
// simplified repro
import fetch from 'undici-fetch';
import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference';

const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

async function send() {
  const ctrl = new AbortController();
  try {
    // note: ctrl is reused across retries by naive app code
    return await client.fetch('/v1/generate', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: JSON.stringify({ prompt: 'order create' }),
      signal: ctrl.signal,
    });
  } catch (err) {
    throw err;
  }
}

send();
```
kevin_support: Customer says when upstream timeout happens, SDK retries but the original POST sometimes still completes server-side and they see 2 orders.

julie_engineer: Reusing an AbortController across retries is a red flag — once aborted signals can be in an inconsistent state for the request lifecycle. SDK should create a fresh signal per attempt or explicitly disallow reusing. But undici's fetch semantics also differ: if you pass the same signal to multiple fetch attempts and call abort between them you can get odd timing.

dan_devx: Also check backoff implementation: if the SDK schedules a retry but doesn't clone the request body stream properly, you can end up sending partial/duplicated requests. In our earlier bug we saw duplicate POSTs only when body was a stream. Is the customer using a plain string or a stream?

kevin_support: plain JSON string. Logs show sequence: initial request, timeout at proxy (client side), SDK logs "retrying attempt=2 after=250ms", then we see a second POST a moment later. Server logs show first POST completed 120ms after apparent timeout due to buffering in the proxy — so race window.

maria_sre: Ok that lines up with a timing race where client gives up (timeout) but server still processes the first. The only surefire fix is idempotency on server side (idempotency-key) or make client retry safe: use new idempotency token per logical operation or ensure request is not duplicated.

kevin_support: Customer can't change server to be idempotent yet. Can we change SDK behavior to avoid duplicate POSTs when signal abort/timeout triggers?

julie_engineer: Short term workaround: ensure apps do NOT reuse AbortController for retries and let SDK accept an option to auto-generate idempotency keys for non-idempotent endpoints. Medium term: SDK should create a fresh AbortController per attempt if user passes one, or explicitly document: "do not reuse signals across retries."

dan_devx: I dug into SDK 1.11.0 — we have an issue where if user supplies `signal` we pass it through to every attempt. That's wrong. We should: if signal provided and not aborted, wrap it for each attempt so a single abort cancels all attempts, but timeouts/internal retry aborts shouldn't reuse the previous attempt's signal state.

kevin_support: Can you reproduce locally? I can forward customer's logs and a minimized repro.

dan_devx: I reproduced with small script that reuses AbortController and toggles a tiny artificial proxy delay. Repro steps:
1) Start a simple HTTP server that delays response by 300ms for the first request.
2) Client sets SDK timeout = 100ms and retries=2 with exponential backoff.
3) Reuse AbortController across requests.
Expected: either one request processed or client avoids sending second if first still in flight. Actual: second POST arrives and server processes both.

kevin_support: Thanks — customer confirmed they can reproduce with our script.

maria_sre: Proposed fix: create PR to ensure SDK clones/creates a fresh internal signal per attempt. Also add doc and add SDK option `retry.createFreshSignal = true|false` default true. Backwards-compatible.

julie_engineer: Add unit tests mocking undici.fetch that assert we never pass the same signal to two concurrent attempts and that aborted signals don't get reused.

ci-bot: CI pipeline triggered for branch fix/retry-signal-clone — running tests. :gear:

kevin_support: Customer wants ETA and a hotfix. Is this worth a patch release?

dan_devx: Yes — this is a behavior change but it's a critical correctness bug for non-idempotent POSTs. We should release 1.11.1 (patch) or 1.12.0 depending on our semver policy. I'll prepare PR and changelog.

ci-bot: All unit tests passed. Integration tests show one flaky related to streaming bodies (investigating).

dan_devx: Pushed patch to release/v1.11.x, bumped to 1.11.1. Change: internal attempt signal creation + docs + example recommended pattern:
```ts
// recommended: create a fresh AbortController per logical attempt only if you need to abort all
const overall = new AbortController();
await client.requestWithRetry('/v1/create', { body }, { signal: overall.signal });
// library will create per-attempt signals; overall.abort() cancels all
```

kevin_support: I shared v1.11.1 with customer who tested in staging. They reported no duplicate orders after exercising the repro. :tada:

ci-bot: deploy-bot: rolled v1.11.1 to hosted pool (canary) — monitor dashboards.

maria_sre: Follow-up: add KB article for support: "Why retries can duplicate POSTs and how to mitigate (idempotency keys, non-reuse of AbortController, SDK patch)". Also recommend customers set idempotency-key headers for create endpoints.

kevin_support: Closing this thread once docs/Kb live and prod metrics stable. Thanks all! :thumbsup:
